## Stage 0 — `catalogRevision` must track the assembled module set

**Why first.** Until this is fixed, a module that appears mid-session never gets a
strip, so every later stage is partly untestable against a live cluster: you cannot
watch a new probe light up because it never arrives.

### The change

`catalogShape()` (`packages/api/src/systemCore/catalog.ts:960`) is a pure function
of the static `CATALOG` constant, so the revision is a compile-time constant. The
list it is meant to fingerprint is not — `expandCatalog` produces one module per
`probeSuccess` instance and `buildOverflow` appends discovered targets.

Replace it with a function of the modules actually assembled.

```ts
// packages/api/src/systemCore/catalog.ts — replaces catalogShape()

/**
 * A fingerprint of the module *set*.
 *
 * Sent as `catalogRevision` so the client can tell "the module list changed" from
 * "the numbers changed" and reconcile only when it must.
 *
 * Built from the assembled modules rather than from CATALOG, because CATALOG is
 * not the list: `expandCatalog` turns one `probe` entry into one module per
 * blackbox target, and `buildOverflow` appends whatever discovery found. Deriving
 * this from the static array made it a compile-time constant, and the client
 * reconciled exactly once per page load.
 *
 * Every term is something a *measurement* cannot move. An id appearing or
 * disappearing is not a measurement — that is precisely the event this exists to
 * signal.
 */
export function revisionOf(
  modules: readonly Pick<SystemCoreModule, 'id' | 'placement' | 'channels'>[],
): string {
  const parts: string[] = [];
  for (const m of modules) {
    const p = m.placement;
    parts.push(`${m.id}:${p.order}:${p.radius}:${p.arc}:${p.speed}:${m.channels.length}`);
  }
  return parts.join('|');
}
```

