## Stage 4 — The band ticker

Fixed name, `||`, then the readings scrolling right to left. Depends on 1, 2 and 3.

### 4a — Carry the string to the scene

```ts
// objects/Strip.tsx — StripSpec
  /**
   * The scrolling readout, already quantised and separator-terminated.
   * null draws no ticker: nothing resolved, or the scene is holding still.
   */
  ticker: string | null;
```

```ts
// scene/resolve.ts — stripSpec
    ticker: live == null ? null : tickerOf(live.channels),
```

### 4b — Layout

Extend the existing `label` memo (`Strip.tsx:138-158`) to return both slices
rather than adding a second memo — they share `lr`, `lh`, `arc` and `lead`, and
the ticker window is defined by what the name leaves over.

Append the separator to the **name** string rather than giving it geometry of its
own: `labels.get(spec.label + ' ||')`. One less mesh, one less measurement, and the
name cache stays bounded by the module count.

```ts
// scene/config.ts
/** Share of a strip's usable arc the fixed name may take before it shrinks. */
export const NAME_SHARE = 0.4;
/** How fast the readout scrolls, in world units per second. */
export const TICKER_SPEED = 0.09;
/** Below this window width the readout is dropped rather than shown unreadable. */
export const TICKER_MIN_SHARE = 0.25;
```

```ts
const usable = arc * (1 - 2 * LABEL_LEAD);
const winTheta = usable - nameTheta;
// A wide name on a narrow arc leaves no window. Dropping the ticker is the right
// answer: the panel has the same numbers, in a font you can actually read.
if (winTheta < usable * TICKER_MIN_SHARE) {
  return { name, ticker: null };
}
map.wrapS = THREE.RepeatWrapping;
map.repeat.x = (winTheta * lr) / (lh * aspect);
const step = TICKER_SPEED / (lh * aspect); // uv units per second
```

### 4c — Scroll

One line in the existing `useFrame` (`Strip.tsx:123-130`), sharing its clamped
delta. `MAX_DELTA` applies for the same reason it applies to rotation: returning to
a backgrounded tab must not fling the text.

```ts
  useFrame((_, delta) => {
    const group = groupRef.current;
    if (!group || !animate) {
      return;
    }
    const d = Math.min(delta, MAX_DELTA);
    group.rotation.y += rate * d;
    onPhase(id, group.rotation.y);
    const map = ticker?.material.map;
    if (map) {
      map.offset.x += ticker.step * d;
    }
  });
```

### 4d — Carry the offset across a content swap

Without this the ticker snaps back to the start every time a number changes — three
times a minute. In an effect, not in the memo: seeding during render would be a
render-phase write to a cached object.

```ts
const carried = useRef(0);
useEffect(() => {
  const map = ticker?.material.map;
  if (!map) {
    return;
  }
  map.offset.x = carried.current % 1;
  return () => {
    carried.current = map.offset.x;
  };
}, [ticker]);
```

This buys continuity of *motion*, not of *content*: the string changed length, so
which word sits under the viewport cannot be preserved. It removes the snap; it
does not remove the swap.

### 4e — Reduced motion

`animate` is false under `prefers-reduced-motion` (`Scene.tsx:281`) and there is no
`useFrame`, so a ticker would freeze mid-string and read as broken text. **Skip the
mesh entirely** — `spec.ticker != null && animate`. Same call `Scene.tsx:130-141`
already makes for the audio bus, and the accessible one: text in motion has to be
pausable. The panel carries the same readings.

